values ('cb050bd9-b7c7-598a-826d-924f6d00f3f7', 'wg8-paragraph-transitions-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g8-paragraph-transitions'), null,
        '6-8', $q$From Push Bike to Bicycle$q$, $q$(1) The bicycle did not appear all at once; it changed shape many times over several decades. (2) The earliest version had two wheels and a seat but no pedals at all. (3) Riders pushed against the ground with their feet, a little like children on scooters. (4) For example, inventors attached pedals directly to the front wheel. (5) This design was faster, but the ride was so rough on stone streets that people nicknamed it the "boneshaker."

(6) To go even faster, makers built a front wheel that was taller than an adult. (7) These high wheelers were thrilling, yet a rider who hit a rock could tumble headfirst over the handlebars. (8) Finally, a safer design arrived with two wheels of the same size and a chain that turned the back wheel. (9) Today's bicycles still follow that basic plan.$q$, 'draft')
